    <h1>How to Estimate Automation ROI Before You Build</h1>
    <div class="post-meta"><span><i class="fas fa-user" aria-hidden="true"></i> Facware Team</span><span><i class="fas fa-calendar-alt" aria-hidden="true"></i> November 11, 2026</span><span><i class="fas fa-clock" aria-hidden="true"></i> 7 min read</span></div>
  </div></div>
  <section class="prose-section" aria-label="Article content"><div class="container"><article class="prose">
    <p class="lead">A useful automation business case does not begin with a software estimate. It begins with a clear, observable cost of the current workflow.</p>
    <h2>Start with the process, not the tool</h2>
    <p>Define the trigger, steps, people, systems, decision points, frequency, and exceptions. If the process varies widely, break it into a predictable core flow and its exceptions instead of averaging everything into one number.</p>
    <h2>Calculate the current effort</h2>
    <p>Estimate the time per occurrence, occurrences per period, and fully loaded cost for the people involved. A simple model is: <strong>time per occurrence × occurrences × loaded hourly cost</strong>. Record assumptions beside the calculation so they can be challenged and updated.</p>
    <h2>Include error and delay costs carefully</h2>
    <p>Manual effort is not the only cost. Consider rework, delayed customer responses, missed approvals, inaccurate inventory decisions, and compliance exposure. Use only costs you can explain and support; uncertain impacts belong in a separate scenario rather than being presented as fact.</p>
    <h2>Define what automation changes</h2>
    <p>The target is rarely “zero manual work.” A realistic design may automate data movement, route exceptions, and preserve human approval for decisions that need judgment. Estimate the remaining effort as well as the saved effort.</p>
    <h2>Compare the result with total ownership cost</h2>
    <p>Include discovery, design, development, testing, training, monitoring, and ongoing support. Then compare the expected annual benefit with the first-year cost and state when the estimate will be reviewed after launch.</p>
    <div class="highlight-box"><p><strong>Example framework:</strong> Use conservative, expected, and upside scenarios. The expected scenario should be the decision baseline; the upside scenario should never be treated as a promise.</p></div>
    <h2>Make the estimate useful</h2>
    <p>The goal is a decision, not a perfect forecast. A documented model helps leaders compare automation opportunities and decide which one deserves discovery first. Try the <a href="/#roi-calculator">ROI calculator</a>, review <a href="/services/process-automation.html">process automation services</a>, or <a href="/contact.html#contact-form" data-assessment-cta>book a free operations assessment</a>.</p>
